  "final_critic_review_md": "# Editorial review\n\n**Disposition: accept.**\n\nI re-read the complete candidate and the pre-rewrite baseline, including the unchanged arguments. **I found no remaining mathematical gap, substantive regression, changed parameter class, or weakened conclusion.** The exact candidate establishes matching upper and lower bounds for the original problem:\n\\[\n\\mathcal R_n(\\alpha,r)\\asymp\nn^{-2\\alpha/(2\\alpha+d+3)}\n\\]\nin **squared** \\(L^2(D)\\) loss, for every \\(d\\ge2\\), every \\(\\alpha>1\\), and every sufficiently small fixed \\(r>0\\), including integer Hölder–Zygmund indices.\n\nThe final formatting repair also succeeds: my independent two-run compilation produced **15 pages**, with **no errors, warnings, unresolved references, or overfull/underfull boxes**.\n\n## 1. Earlier findings: current status\n\n| Earlier finding | Assessment of the current candidate |\n|---|---|\n| The variance lemma needed bounded classical first derivatives, not merely a Zygmund \\(C^1\\)-norm. | **Resolved.** Lemma 2.4 explicitly assumes classical continuous differentiability and bounds \\(a,\\nabla a\\). |\n| That stronger hypothesis needed verification for \\(a=f_h\\). | **Resolved.** Equations (2.2) and (3.5) establish the required uniform bound. |\n| The explanatory smoothing paragraph confused classical derivatives with integer Zygmund spaces. | **Resolved.** It now expressly requires bounded continuous classical derivatives. Lemma 2.1 uses the same distinction. |\n| The kernel-construction citation was too specifically attached to the dilate construction. | **Resolved.** Tsybakov is now cited only for background on higher-order kernels. |\n| The related-work comparison omitted stronger smoothness assumptions. | **Resolved.** The integer threshold and boundary restrictions are now stated. |\n| Fano’s conclusion needed to concern average classification error. | **Resolved and retained.** |\n| The last mixed-frequency paragraph remained overfull. | **Resolved in the exact candidate.** The adopted wording preserves the derivative estimate and compiles cleanly. |\n\nNo substantive earlier objection remains applicable, and I found no new required correction.\n\n## 2. Full mathematical audit\n\n### Statement, model, and quantifiers\n\nThe candidate preserves the original statistical experiment and the full conductivity class. In particular:\n\n- \\(r\\) is fixed, not allowed to shrink with \\(n\\);\n- the upper bound is uniform over the entire prescribed ball;\n- no boundary values of \\(f\\) are assumed known;\n- integer smoothness indices retain their Hölder–Zygmund meaning;\n- the conclusion concerns squared loss, not expected norm or root mean squared error.\n\nThe weak-solution and isonormal-process definitions clarify the setup without adding assumptions. The stated dependence of constants and sufficiently-large-\\(n\\) thresholds is consistent with the proof.\n\n### Lemma 2.1: perturbative regularity\n\nThe noninteger Schauder step has the required smooth domain, zero Dirichlet data, and uniformly elliptic constant-coefficient operator. The cited Gilbarg–Trudinger results cover the existence, a priori estimate, and higher boundary regularity used here. ([djvu.online](https://djvu.online/file/jxRRleAzbYjsl))\n\nThe integer-index passage is a valid argument, not an unresolved appeal to a missing theorem. For an integer \\(\\alpha>1\\), interpolate the same operator\n\\[\nA=\\Delta_D^{-1}\\operatorname{div}\n\\]\nbetween the nearby noninteger bounds\n\\[\nA:C^{\\alpha\\pm\\varepsilon}(D)\n\\longrightarrow C^{\\alpha+1\\pm\\varepsilon}(D).\n\\]\nThe domain-extension framework permits interpolation of the restriction spaces; the already-defined Dirichlet operator retains its zero boundary trace. The cited Triebel sections concern precisely interpolation, extensions, and spaces on smooth domains. ([link.springer.com](https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/bfm%3A978-3-0346-0419-2/1))\n\nThe algebra estimate then supplies the contraction. The strict inequalities \\(1<\\alpha\\) and \\(2<\\alpha+1\\) give the bounded classical derivatives needed later. The source `FLAG` records a reference-locator limitation, not a missing mathematical step.\n\n### Lemma 2.2: product-smoothing cancellation\n\nAll three frequency regimes remain justified.\n\n- **Low–low:** the double-difference identity has the correct sign. Moment cancellation supports Taylor expansion in the bandwidth, and the first-difference argument supplies the essential lower-frequency factor \\(2^i\\).\n- **Mixed:** the proof explicitly uses\n  \\[\n  \\|D^2F_{\\rm lo}\\|_\\infty\\lesssim h^{-1}\n  \\]\n  when taking two derivatives of the commutator. Thus it does not improperly rely on a Lipschitz bound alone.\n- **High–high:** differentiated kernels give \\(O(h^{2\\alpha-k})\\), which is bounded by the claimed order because \\(\\alpha>1\\).\n\nThe Bernstein reference is appropriate for the block derivative estimates. ([statslab.cam.ac.uk](https://www.statslab.cam.ac.uk/~rn289/Site/__files/FULLPDF.pdf?utm_source=openai))\n\nThe summations also justify the integer endpoints without a logarithm. The final wording change affects only line breaking: the estimate for the differentiated first product and its dependence on the preceding bounds are unchanged.\n\n### Lemma 2.3: transport inversion\n\nThe convexity and outward-drift assumptions give the required invariant backward flow and unique interior minimum. The characteristic formula has the correct sign, and boundedness eliminates its terminal term. No transport boundary condition is missing.\n\nThe \\(L^2\\) argument correctly changes variables onto \\(\\Phi_t(\\Omega)\\), not onto all of \\(\\Omega\\). The resulting inequality gives the stated operator bound.\n\nThe smoothness justification remains sufficient after rewriting: polynomial bounds on flow derivatives, multiplied by the exponential factor retained in derivatives of \\(J_t\\), make the differentiated integrals converge. The gradient differential inequality has the correct direction. Its tensor version used below follows by the same finite-dimensional norm argument.\n\n### Lemma 2.4: variance gain\n\nThe lemma now assumes exactly the classical coefficient bounds its covariance calculation uses. Determinism of both \\(v\\) and \\(a\\) is explicit.\n\nThe trajectory-separation estimate follows from contraction toward the minimum and does not assume straight characteristics. The time integration uses the valid inequality\n\\[\n2c_0=1>\\frac{3}{2d}=M.\n\\]\nThe critical-point neighborhood is included in the spatial integration:\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Omega_h}\\min\\{1,h/|x-x_v|\\}\\,dx\n\\lesssim h^d+h\\int_h^2\\rho^{d-2}\\,d\\rho\n\\lesssim h.\n\\]\nConsequently, the asserted \\(n^{-1}h^{-d-3}\\) variance bound is justified uniformly, without a hidden derivative assumption or logarithmic loss.\n\n### Upper bound: estimator and boundary treatment\n\nThe gate is observable and ensures the hypotheses of the transport inverse. The deterministic margins in (3.4) follow from the classical derivative bounds; the signed kernel changes only constants.\n\nThe radial extrapolation retains both necessary properties:\n\n1. a uniform \\(L^2\\) operator bound;\n2. an \\(O(h^\\alpha)\\) approximation error, including integer indices through the dyadic argument.\n\nThus the estimator and risk calculation cover **all of \\(D\\)**. No boundary information about \\(f\\) is inserted through the extension.\n\nMeasurability is adequately justified by smooth versions of the observed fields, finite-time flow dependence, and limits of the characteristic integrals. Clipping decreases the loss and controls the exceptional event.\n\n### Upper bound: residual, randomness, and remainder\n\nThe residual identity (3.6) is exact, and interior kernel support excludes boundary terms.\n\nThe Gaussian-event calculation has the correct derivative and canonical-distance scaling. The cited concentration theorem applies to the separable, bounded smoothed Gaussian processes under consideration. ([statslab.cam.ac.uk](https://www.statslab.cam.ac.uk/~rn289/Site/__files/FULLPDF.pdf?utm_source=openai))\n\nThe bounds for \\(g\\) and \\(\\nabla g\\) account for every differentiated forcing term, including \\(D^2f_h\\,\\nabla e\\) and the coefficient derivative in the transport gradient bound.\n\nMost importantly, (3.12) remains an **exact expansion around the deterministic inverse** \\(T_{u_h}^{-1}\\). The variance lemma is therefore applied legitimately. The nonlinear term is bounded pathwise, without assuming independence between \\(g\\) and \\(e\\).\n\nThe final comparisons are correct:\n\\[\nn^{-1}h^{-d-3}=h^{2\\alpha},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{(\\log n)^2h^{4\\alpha-2}}{h^{2\\alpha}}\n=(\\log n)^2h^{2\\alpha-2}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nTogether with clipping and extrapolation, this proves the full uniform upper bound.\n\n### Lower bound: construction and class membership\n\nThe angular bump counts, derivative bounds, and Laplacian norms have the correct dimension-dependent powers. Disjoint supports prevent an unwanted multiplicity factor in supremum estimates.\n\nThe prescribed solutions satisfy the exact boundary condition and transport geometry. The derivative induction for \\(q_\\theta\\) is noncircular: the principal tensor coefficient is bounded below by \\(c_0+km\\), and every remaining product contains a strictly lower derivative of \\(q_\\theta\\).\n\nThe resulting classical derivative estimates imply the required Hölder–Zygmund restriction-norm bound, including at integer \\(\\alpha\\). Choosing fixed \\(\\kappa\\) sufficiently small gives membership in the original class. Elliptic uniqueness establishes\n\\[\nu_\\theta=u_{f_\\theta}\n\\]\nexactly.\n\n### Lower bound: separation and information\n\nThe nonlinear remainder remains uniformly \\(O(\\kappa^2h^{2\\alpha})\\).\n\nThe candidate’s enlargement of the tail annulus to \\(1/2<\\rho<1\\) is valid: the radial profile is compactly supported inside \\((1/4,1/2)\\). The radial inverse and integration identity are correct. Angular separation dominates the nonlinear remainder for sufficiently small \\(h\\).\n\nThe KL calculation uses exact solution differences, not a linear approximation. Its Gaussian-shift formula agrees with the cited equation (6.16). ([statslab.cam.ac.uk](https://www.statslab.cam.ac.uk/~rn289/Site/__files/FULLPDF.pdf?utm_source=openai))\n\nThe packing-information balance is\n\\[\n\\frac{nh^{2\\alpha+4}}{h^{-(d-1)}}=nh^{2\\alpha+d+3}.\n\\]\nThe packing and average-error Fano references are appropriate. The explicit mutual-information identity also correctly permits a reference distribution outside the selected packing. Nearest-member classification yields the claimed squared-risk lower bound. ([studylib.net](https://studylib.net/doc/25650939/introduction-to-nonparametric-estimation-by-alexandre-b.-...))\n\n## 3. References and clarity\n\nThe corrected related-work comparison states the smoothness threshold and distinguishes expected norm from squared loss. Its rate conversion from noise level \\(n^{-1/2}\\) is correct. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.08818))\n\nThe kernel citation now accurately serves as background rather than attribution of the particular construction. The Richter and Koers–Szabó–van der Vaart attributions support the historical and characteristic-flow discussion. ([studylib.net](https://studylib.net/doc/25650939/introduction-to-nonparametric-estimation-by-alexandre-b.-...)) The descriptions of Bayesian and information-theoretic work are likewise consistent with the cited sources. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07343))\n\nThe candidate is substantially longer than the baseline, but its organization makes the proof dependencies clearer. In particular, it explains the integer endpoints, deterministic/random distinction, boundary extrapolation, and solution-first lower bound without removing essential reasoning. The compressed passages remain readable.\n\n## 4. Independent LaTeX check\n\nI reconstructed the exact submitted source without edits and ran `pdflatex` twice.\n\n| Contract requirement | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Complete standalone document | **Pass** |\n| Exactly `\\documentclass[12pt]{article}` | **Pass** |\n| At most 16 pages | **Pass: 15 pages** |\n| No prohibited layout, spacing, or explicit font-size changes | **Pass** |\n| Successful compilation | **Pass: both runs exited successfully** |\n| Resolved references and citations | **Pass** |\n| Final-run warnings or overfull/underfull boxes | **None** |\n\nI also inspected the rendered page 6 and an overview of all fifteen pages. The previously overflowing mixed-frequency paragraph now fits within the margins.\n\n**No required mathematical, reference, wording, or formatting repair remains.**\n\n<cleanup_verdict>accept</cleanup_verdict>\n\n",
